# UBSan suppressions for the port's sanitizer run (tests/port/run.py --sanitize; psxstack's port_test passes the file
# as UBSAN_OPTIONS=suppressions=...). psxstack's docs/PORT.md "Compiling the game C for the host": only out-of-bounds
# indexes of the game's own C that stay inside one struct whose layout is the same on the host, so the host computes
# what the PS1 computes. Each line names the function and the finding (docs/PORT.md "Testing").
#
# assignCardCopySerial (src/main/card/card_db.c), from resetPlayerData's j < 8: each card gets 8 serials in a
# u16 cardCopySerials[301][6] row, so copies 6 and 7 land on the next card's copies 0 and 1 (which that card's own
# turn rewrites) and the last card's on areaScriptFlags[0] (which resetScriptProgress zeroes after). Both inside
# PlayerProfile, whose bytes from bonusCounts to unk2435 have the PS1's layout on the host (port/game/state.c).
bounds:assignCardCopySerial
#
# KAW_tickCardCursor (src/kawseg/duel/kaw_hand.c): the card cursor looks for the nearest card through 9 entries from
# Player.hand[4] on, so hand[4..8] are topCards[0..4], the s8/u8 bytes right after it (Player 0x1B9-0x1C1, pointer-free:
# the same layout on the host). Reached in the duel (issue #30), not by the four gated scripts.
bounds:KAW_tickCardCursor
#
# runDuelTurnLoop (src/main/duel/duel.c), step 30: the support card under the cursor, read as
# PLAYER(ME)->hand[DUEL->cursorSlot] with cursorSlot 4 when the player picks the Online Deck's top card: hand[4] is
# topCards[0], the same bytes as KAW_tickCardCursor's (the value is not used for slot 4). Reached by first_duel_play.
bounds:runDuelTurnLoop
#
# SUG_tickHudSlides (src/sugseg/battle/sug_hud.c): state 2 compares the slide with target[state], so target[2] is
# trail[0] (HudSlide: float, then s16s, pointer-free: the same layout on the host). Reached in every polygon battle's
# HUD, first by first_duel_play.
bounds:SUG_tickHudSlides
